// core: what the binary provides. Every relation here is `.input`: the Rerail binary supplies its rows, and
// there are no rules for it. Every other library is Datalog over these.
// Tape relations are read in place from the parse tape (decision 0026); none is a table of rows per node.
//
// The language is Soufflé Datalog. Rules, `.decl`, `.input`, `.output`, `.type`, `.functor`, `#include`,
// `!`, `_`, `count : { … }`, `.lattice`, `.pragma`, `match`, `contains` and `@functors` mean what they mean in
// Soufflé. docs/rule-syntax/ALIGNMENT.md records how this matches the other branches' notations (the `.rdl`
// dialect, the JSON policies). Six additions, each described where it is used:
//   1. Patterns:  n ~ `code pattern`  matches a node against the language's grammar (matcher atoms, below).
//   2. Captures:  named regex groups in match("…(?<X>…)…", s) bind X (value functions and captures, below).
//   3. must, may: three-valued relations, for facts that may be unknown (the tsx and python libraries).
//   4. Findings and fixes: .violation, fix, `=> edits` and .edit (edits, below; the page's Fixes section).
//   5. join: an ordered string aggregate, `s = join x by k sep "," : { … }` (order and aggregation, below).
//   6. ordered by: recursion through `!` and aggregates that only looks back along source order (same section).
// Plus the policy directives (.policy, .mode, .scope, .budget, .gate, …), which are settings, not logic
// (findings and enforcement, at the end). Everything else the data-layer checks need is relations and functors:
// docs/rule-syntax/data-layer/DESIGN.md maps each of the 142 checks to what it uses.
//
// Names used below: `d` a document id, `n`/`p`/`c`/`a` nodes, `s`/`v`/`k` symbols.

// ---- types -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// A node is a syntax-tree node: an id on the tape. A span is a piece of one node's text, made by a regex capture
// (see `match` below). Relations that read source text (text, line, the edits) take either: `loc`.
.type node <: number
.type span <: number
.type loc = node | span

// ---- the tape ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Facts about documents. A document is one parsed source file; `d` is its integer id.
// doc(d, "src/app/main.py", "app.main"): the module name is derived from the path; "" when the file has none.
.decl doc(d: number, path: symbol, module: symbol)  // one row per document; module is the dotted module name, or ""
// pkg(d, "app") for src/app/main.py: the dotted Python package holding the document.
.decl pkg(d: number, p: symbol)                          // the package a document belongs to (Python)
// For "src/app/main.py": path_part(d, "src"), path_part(d, "app"), path_part(d, "main.py"). Lets rules test
// "is this file under a `tests` directory" without regex on the whole path.
.decl path_part(d: number, s: symbol)                    // each directory of the document's path, and its file name
// file_name(d, "main.py") for the same document.
.decl file_name(d: number, s: symbol)                    // the last component of the document's path
// Every node of every document, with its grammar kind and byte range [start, end). Offsets are what the
// tree relations below compare: a is an ancestor of n exactly when a's range encloses n's.
.decl node(d: number, n: node, kind: symbol, start: number, end: number)
// kind(n, "call_expression"): the tree-sitter node type. Kind names are the grammar's own (see .language).
.decl kind(n: node, k: symbol)                           // a node's grammar kind
// In `f(x)`: field(call, "function", f) and field(call, "arguments", args). Field names come from the grammar.
.decl field(p: node, f: symbol, c: node)                 // c is p's child in field f
// In `f(a, b)`, the argument list has child(args, a, 0) and child(args, b, 1). Anonymous tokens (`(`, `,`)
// are not named children and are not counted. Comments are named children (tree-sitter's extras) and are
// counted: in `f(# c` / `a)`, child(args, comment, 0), child(args, a, 1). Where an index must not count them,
// the libraries filter them (`!kind(c, "comment")`, python/ast's body_stmt, tsx's call_arg).
.decl child(p: node, c: node, i: number)                 // c is p's i-th named child, from 0
// text(n, "foo") for the identifier `foo`; text(call, "f(a, b)") for the call. Every node's and span's source
// text exactly as written, comments and spacing inside it included. Interned, so equal texts compare as equal
// symbols cheaply. For an identifier's or a dotted name's text, `name` (below) is the typed relation.
.decl text(n: loc, s: symbol)                            // source text, interned
// Every anonymous token child of n, punctuation included: in `a + b`, op(binary, "+"); in `a not in b`,
// op(cmp, "not in") (one token); in `async def f()`, op(def, "async"); in `f(a, b)`,
// op(args, ","); in `static readonly x`, op(field, "static") and op(field, "readonly"). These tokens have no
// node of their own. op_at (below) is the ordered view.
.decl op(n: node, s: symbol)                             // an anonymous token child: operators, keywords, punctuation
// docof(n, d): the document node n was parsed from. Joins a node to doc(d, path, module).
.decl docof(n: loc, d: number)
// line(n, 12): used in messages ("at line {outer.line}") and line-based suppressions.
.decl line(n: loc, l: number)                            // 1-based start line
// unset(x): one row, the empty value. A rule translated from ast-grep binds a metavariable that its match left
// empty to it: an `any` alternative that does not mention $X, as ast-grep allows. text(x, "") holds, and no
// tree relation has it, so `kind(X, …)` on an empty metavariable is false, as in ast-grep.
.decl unset(x: loc)
// list_len(l, k): list variable l (a $$$X of a pattern) has k nodes. For a JSON `count` transform.
.decl list_len(l: loc, k: number)
// value(n, "a\nb") for the literal "a\\nb"; value(n, "255") for 0xff. Lets rules compare literals by meaning.
.decl value(n: node, v: symbol)  // static value of a literal: strings unescaped, numbers normalised
// str(n, "react") for the literal 'react' or "react": the decoded contents (escapes applied; a raw literal keeps
// its backslashes), without prefix or quotes, as decision 0037 has it. A literal with interpolations has no row:
// a Python f-string with `{x}`, a JavaScript template with `${x}`, a PHP "…$x…". Their constant pieces are
// str_part (Python, below).
.decl str(n: node, v: symbol)                            // a string literal's decoded contents
// strprefix(n, "rb") for rb"…": the literal's prefix letters, lowercased; "" for none.
.decl strprefix(n: node, p: symbol)
// name(n, "a.b.c"): an identifier's text, or the dotted text of an attribute chain or dotted name, no spaces.
// The usual subject of a text test: names hold only word characters and dots.
.decl name(n: node, s: symbol)
// comment(n, "noqa: E501"): a comment's text without its marker (`#`, `//`).
.decl comment(n: node, s: symbol)
// With `const M = "react"; import(M)`, static_value(arg, "react"). Follows only constants in the same file.
.decl static_value(n: node, v: symbol)                   // value after following file-local constant bindings
// value_type("12", "int") for Python, "number" for TypeScript: the type of a value produced by `value`.
.decl value_type(v: symbol, t: symbol)                   // "str", "int", "string", "number", …
.input doc, pkg, path_part, file_name, node, kind, field, child, text, op, docof, line, unset, list_len
.input value, str, strprefix, name, comment, static_value, value_type

// ---- text tests (built-in constraints) -----------------------------------------------------------------------
// Most tests on text are not regexes: exact names, sets of names, prefixes, suffixes, path shapes. They are
// written as globs, with built-in constraints shaped like Soufflé's match(re, s) and contains(sub, s): pattern
// first, subject second, both bound; they bind nothing.
//   glob(p, s)      s matches glob p: `*` any run without `/`, `**` any run, `?` one character other than `/`,
//                   `[A-Z]` / `[0-9_]` one listed character, `{a,b}` alternatives, `\` escapes the next character
//   prefix(p, s)    s starts with p;  suffix(p, s): ends with p;  contains(sub, s): Soufflé's own
//   word(w, s)      w occurs in s as whole words, any separators between them: "on delete" in "ON  DELETE x"
//   iglob, iprefix, isuffix, icontains, iword: the same, ignoring case
// A set of patterns is a relation: `cache_pat(p), glob(p, s)` is true when any of them matches. The names are
// reserved: no relation may be declared glob, prefix, suffix, contains, word or an `i` form.
// Each distinct string is tested once per run, for every rule together (decision 0037): the engine compiles each
// group of tests to a hash set, an Aho-Corasick automaton or a small DFA over the interned vocabulary, and rules
// join on symbol ids. A test never runs per row.
//
// match(re, s) stays for what a glob cannot say: named captures, which bind variables, and real regular
// expressions (entity references, attribute grammars in the web packs). The dialect is a DFA's: no look-around,
// no back-references, ASCII \b. Captures are taken after the match, once per distinct string. The translator
// writes a glob test wherever one is exactly the regex: 1,949 of 2,123 distinct patterns in the packs and ports.

// ---- source text (every document, parsed or not) ------------------------------------------------------------
// Checks that count lines, scan comments or read files with no grammar (Markdown, Rust read as text) need the
// raw bytes, comments and blank lines included. A document in `.scope text "**/*.md"` has doc, path_part and these
// relations, and no tree.
// source(d, sp): the whole file as one span. match / match_all over it give spans with lines.
.decl source(d: number, sp: span)
// src_line(d, 12, sp): physical line 12, without its terminator. Lines end at \n, \r\n or a lone \r: the
// terminators CPython's tokenizer, Ruff and ESLint count, so src_line numbers agree with line(n). A form feed is
// whitespace inside a line (str.splitlines() would split there, and disagree with every tool).
// A Datalog version over match_all would be exact for \n only, so it is native.
.decl src_line(d: number, l: number, sp: span)
// split_line follows Python str.splitlines(), for text scanners: CRLF is one boundary;
// LF, CR, VT, FF, FS, GS, RS, NEL, U+2028 and U+2029 also split. Empty input has no rows.
// A terminal boundary adds no extra row. Text is complete, including lines longer than 256 bytes.
// These logical line numbers are separate from AST coordinates. Prepared only when demanded.
.decl split_line(d: number, l: number, t: symbol)
// LF-only lines, as Python str.split('\n'): preserve CR and other Unicode separators.
// Empty input and a terminal LF each retain an empty line. Made only when demanded.
.decl lf_line(d: number, l: number, t: symbol)
.decl line_count(d: number, k: number)                   // number of physical lines, as src_line counts them
.decl end_line(n: loc, l: number)                        // 1-based line of the last character
.decl col(n: loc, c: number)                             // 0-based column of the start, for keys that need one
// op_at(n, i, s): n's operator tokens in order, from 0: in `a < b <= c`, op_at(cmp, 0, "<"), op_at(cmp, 1, "<=").
// A two-word operator is one token: "not in", "is not". The tokens are n's anonymous children other than
// brackets, quotes, `,`, `;`, `:` and `.`, whatever field holds them: in a Rust
// `let` chain, op_at(chain, 0, "&&"); in `not x`, op_at(n, 0, "not"). `op` (above) is the unordered view, with
// punctuation; chained comparisons need the order.
.decl op_at(n: node, i: number, s: symbol)
// syntax_error(d, 3, "invalid syntax"): the file does not parse. One row per document, at the first error.
// For Python the verdict and line come from a native parser that follows CPython's grammar (Ruff's parser):
// tree-sitter recovers from errors, so its tree cannot say whether CPython accepts the file. The message is that
// parser's; messages are not part of a finding's key. No CPython runs, in any form. Other languages use
// tree-sitter's ERROR and MISSING nodes. The document is withheld from the policy and its outcome is Unknown(parse); a policy can also report it
// (`.on syntax_error`, at the end).
.decl syntax_error(d: number, l: number, msg: symbol)
.input source, src_line, split_line, lf_line, line_count, end_line, col, op_at, syntax_error
// Tokens: the leaves of the parse tree, named or anonymous, in source order. Comments are not tokens, as in
// ESLint's token store (getTokenBefore, getFirstToken, …), so rules that reason about tokens port directly.
// A token is a span, as a regex capture is: text, line, end_line and col apply to it, a finding can sit on it,
// and an edit can replace it. Read off the tree; no text is scanned.
// In `if (a) b();`: first_token(if, `if`), last_token(if, `;`), token_before(b(), `)`), token_after(`if`, `(`).
.decl first_token(n: node, t: span)                     // n's first token
.decl last_token(n: node, t: span)                      // n's last token
.decl token_before(x: loc, t: span)                     // the token just before node or token x
.decl token_after(x: loc, t: span)                      // the token just after node or token x
.input first_token, last_token, token_before, token_after

// ---- tree structure (tape arithmetic, never Datalog recursion) -------------------------------------------------
// These answer ancestor and sibling questions from node offsets. A recursive `ancestor` relation in Datalog
// would materialise every (node, ancestor) pair; on the tape each question is a range comparison.
// In `function f() { g(); }`: within(call_g, body), within(call_g, f), but not within(f, call_g).
.decl within(n: node, a: node)                           // n is strictly below a
// Cost: a range test per pair, but its rows are every (node, ancestor) pair. Bind both ends, or use inside/nearest.
// inside(n, a, set): a is a strict ancestor of n, and a's kind is in set. has(a, n, set): n is a strict descendant
// of a, and n's kind is in set. So has is not inside with its columns swapped: has(a, n, set) is
// within(n, a), kind(n, k), set(k). The set always constrains the end being looked for (decision 0036):
// inside(call, f, "function_definition"): call is somewhere in function f. !has(b, _, "comment"): no comment
// anywhere inside b. The kind set is part of the axis, so the transducer answers either in one tape pass.
// Cost (inside, has, nearest, within_stop, precedes_stop): one tape pass per axis and set; keep the set small.
.decl inside(n: node, a: node, set: relation)
.decl has(a: node, n: node, set: relation)
//
// inside, has, within_stop, precedes_stop and nearest take a set as their last argument: the name of a unary relation of kinds
// (`scope`, `fdef`) or of nodes (`render_stop`), or one kind written as a string ("program"). This is the one
// place a relation is passed as an argument, which Soufflé has no form for; the alternative, a recursive climb
// per stop set, is what these relations exist to avoid.
// within_stop(call, f, fn_scope): call is inside f with no nested function in between, i.e. f is the call's
// own function, not an outer one. Lowers JSON's `inside` with a `stopBy` rule.
.decl within_stop(n: node, a: node, stop: relation)      // n below a, and no node of `stop` strictly between them
// nearest(ref, s, block_scope): s is the innermost enclosing block. The set may be a derived relation too
// (tsx/jsx uses nearest(c, p, render_stop)), so "climb until X" needs no recursion.
.decl nearest(n: node, a: node, set: relation)           // a: n's nearest strict ancestor in `set`
// In `a(); b(); c();` as siblings: precedes(a, c) holds; next_sibling(a, b) holds, next_sibling(a, c) does not.
.decl precedes(a: node, b: node)                         // child(p, a, i), child(p, b, j), i < j
.decl next_sibling(a: node, b: node)                     // b is the named sibling right after a
// precedes_stop(a, b, stop): as precedes, with no sibling of `stop` between them. Lowers JSON's `precedes` and
// `follows` with a `stopBy` rule.
.decl precedes_stop(a: node, b: node, stop: relation)
// ---- guests: code inside code (decision 0055) -------------------------------------------------------------
// Python `x: "List[int]"`: parsed(str, "annotation", root) gives the tree of the code inside the string, so
// rules can resolve names in string annotations. A rule's parsed(s, "g", r) or sub_tape(s, "g", t) asks for
// the guest at s (the literals beside it that bind s say which s); a library asks with embed. Each guest is a
// document of its own: its nodes are node rows of its document, its lines and columns are the file's (a decoded
// string's nodes all stand at the string). Cost: one parse per distinct text, cached by text.
// Grammars: "annotation" (a Python expression), or a language label ("python", "sql", "typescript", …).
// Guests have no source, src_line or line_count rows: their text is the host's.
.decl parsed(s: node, grammar: symbol, root: node)       // root of string s parsed as `grammar` ("annotation", …)
.decl sub_tape(at: node, grammar: symbol, t: symbol)     // the tape (content key) of the guest at `at`
.decl guest(g: number, host: number, at: node, grammar: symbol)  // document g is the code at host node `at`
.decl doc_role(d: number, role: symbol)                  // "primary" (a file), "fragment", or an embed's role
.decl doc_root(d: number, n: node)                       // the root of document d's tree
.decl tape_of(d: number, t: symbol)                      // document d places tape t; equal texts share a tape
.decl host_node(n: node, h: node)                        // h is the innermost host node over guest node n
.input sub_tape, guest, doc_role, doc_root, tape_of, host_node
// What a rule sees (decision 0058): files, and guests in the run's language unless `.guests off`. A rule that
// reaches a guest through one of the relations above sees it whatever its grammar, and names its kinds in that
// grammar: guest(g, _, _, "sql"), node(g, n, "object_reference", _, _) is SQL's object_reference ("sql:…" also
// names one). A guest made from a string is parsed from its str: the decoded text, placed through the escapes.
// Tape operations, made on demand in the chunk that asks (value documents: no host, their own lines):
//   t = @parse("sql", q)     parse_of(q, "sql", t)     q parsed as SQL; tape_of(d, t) gives its document
//   x = @unparse(t)          unparse_of(t, x)          a guest's or a value's text
//   t3 = @splice(t, n, t2)   splice_of(t, n, t2, t3)   t with node n's text replaced by t2's, parsed again
.decl parse_of(x: symbol, g: symbol, t: symbol)
.decl unparse_of(t: symbol, x: symbol)
.decl splice_of(t: symbol, n: node, t2: symbol, t3: symbol)
.input parse_of, unparse_of, splice_of
// embed(d, at, "sql", "query"): a library declares that the code at node `at` of document d is a guest of that
// grammar and role (`.guests off` leaves these out). embed_value(d, at, g, role, text) for a computed text,
// which stands at `at`. Rules derive them; the engine makes the guests.
.decl embed(d: number, at: node, grammar: symbol, role: symbol)
.decl embed_value(d: number, at: node, grammar: symbol, role: symbol, text: symbol)
// ---- the environment and the files beyond the corpus (t21) ----
// env(k, v): the run's settings (`rerail.env` at the corpus root, `--env k=v`). dir_entry(dir, name, is_dir) and
// file_line(path, i, text) (from 1): the entries of a directory and the lines of a file, read only for a dir or path
// a rule binds, under the corpus root or a directory a setting names. load(path, module, grammar): a rule asks for
// the file as a document, named as `module` (its resolution's answer); loaded(path, module, grammar): the documents
// loaded, by canonical path. A loaded document is evaluated like any other; its rows are not the run's findings.
.decl env(key: symbol, value: symbol)
// Demanded host inputs, read once per run. "cpu_count" is the online CPU count;
// "env:NAME" reads that process environment variable. Missing or unknown keys have no row.
.decl host_info(key: symbol, value: symbol)
.decl dir_entry(dir: symbol, name: symbol, is_dir: number)
// Metadata shares dir_entry's cached listing. is_file and bytes follow a link;
// is_symlink describes the entry itself. Missing link targets have zero bytes.
.decl dir_metadata(dir: symbol, name: symbol, is_file: number, is_symlink: number, bytes: number)
.decl file_line(path: symbol, i: number, text: symbol)
.decl load(path: symbol, module: symbol, grammar: symbol)
.decl loaded(path: symbol, module: symbol, grammar: symbol)
// spread(n) for `*args` in `f(*args)` and `...xs` in `[...xs]`: positions after a spread are not fixed, so
// argument-matching rules must treat the call as open.
.decl spread(n: node)                                    // a spread element or argument (`*args`, `...xs`)
// split_dot("os.path.join", "os.path", "join"): q is m.x, split at the last dot. A relation, not a function,
// so it runs whichever way round its columns are bound (see @dot below for building a name).
.decl split_dot(q: symbol, m: symbol, x: symbol)
// earlier(m, n): m comes before n in the language's AST visit order, in the same document. For Python that is
// CPython's ast order: a def's decorators and return annotation come after its body, defaults after all the
// parameters. Elsewhere it is start offset. It is the order `ordered by` relations look back along (below), and
// "the latest assignment before this use" is `max` over it.
// Cost: earlier is a comparison of visit_index values, not a stored pair relation; `max i : { …, earlier(m, n),
// visit_index(m, i) }` is set-based. Only relations declared `ordered by earlier` run sequentially per document.
.decl earlier(m: node, n: node)
.decl visit_index(n: node, i: number)                    // the same order as a number, from 0 per document
.decl ast_depth(n: node, k: number)                      // depth in the language's AST, for breadth-first "first"
// ---- ordered first match (data-layer/DESIGN.md, addition 7) ----
// dfs_first(r, hit, first, edge, target): hit is the first node of `target` that a depth-first search from r
// reaches. edge(src, dst, ord) is any relation of three columns; a node's edges are tried in increasing ord
// (then dst), each node is entered once, and a node on the current path is skipped. A target is a hit when an
// edge reaches it; r itself is never its own hit (write `target(r)` beside it for that). first is the ord of
// r's edge that starts the path, so `visit_index(c, first)` gives the call to report. No row: no target is
// reachable. The engine computes it set-based (addition 7); only cycles need a search.
// A known answer or a node to avoid is a rewrite of `edge`, not an extra argument: drop an avoided node's edges
// (keep edges into it if it is a target); give a node with a known answer one edge, to that target.
// Cost: four set passes (reach, first edge into a reaching node, chain ends, cycle components), then a search only
// where a root's chain of first edges enters a cycle. Spike: 1,000,000 nodes and 5,000,000 edges in 0.2 s CPU of
// set passes and 0.06 s for every root (spikes/dfs-first). A cycle through most of the graph keeps the search.
.decl dfs_first(r: node, hit: node, first: number, edge: relation, target: relation)
// dfs_first_memo(…, roots): the scripts' variant. Roots are searched in increasing order of roots' second
// column (a number, or a symbol compared as text, for `sorted(symbol)`),
// sharing one memo, and a node finished while a cycle was open is remembered as reaching nothing. Use it
// only where a finding must match a script that does this; it reports fewer findings on cycles.
// Cost: as dfs_first, plus a search in root order for each root whose chain enters a cycle.
.decl dfs_first_memo(r: node, hit: node, first: number, edge: relation, target: relation, roots: relation)

// bfs_walk(r, hit, ordinal, edge, roots): breadth-first reachable vertices for
// every root in unary roots(r), using edge(src, dst, order). Neighbours follow
// (order, destination id); ordinal starts at zero for each root. A root is not
// pre-visited, so a cycle can reach it. Repeated arrivals produce one row.
// The whole root demand and adjacency are batches, not per-node SQL callbacks.
// IDs retain the edge relation's types, including numbers and symbols.
.decl bfs_walk(r: node, hit: node, ordinal: number, edge: relation, roots: relation)
// ordered_rows(a, b, ordinal, rows): rows has two scalar columns. Distinct rows
// sort by their typed values (symbols as text), with one zero-based global rank.
// One SQL window sorts the whole batch. Empty input produces no rows.
.decl ordered_rows(a: node, b: node, ordinal: number, rows: relation)
// balanced_rows(task, row, bin, ordinal, rows, limits): rows(task, occurrence,
// key, weight), limits(task, bins). Per task, sort by decreasing nonnegative
// weight then key text then occurrence. Assign to the least-loaded bin, ties
// by bin index. Emit in bin and append order, with zero-based task ordinals.
// Occurrences are unique per task; equal keys remain separate occurrences.
// All requested tasks are one batch; tasks absent from limits produce no rows.
.decl balanced_rows(task: node, row: number, bin: number, ordinal: number, rows: relation, limits: relation)
// components(d, c, k, edge, own): document d's strongly connected component of the graph edge(d, e) (d depends on
// e), named by its smallest document c, and the component's key k: the SHA-256 of its documents' digests own(d, h),
// sorted, then the keys of the components it depends on, sorted. Equal keys mean equal digests over the whole import
// closure (decision 0076). A document named only by an edge has the empty digest.
// Cost: linear in the edges (one Tarjan pass; keys bottom up).
.decl components(d: number, c: number, k: symbol, edge: relation, own: relation)
.input within, inside, has, within_stop, nearest, precedes, next_sibling, precedes_stop, parsed, spread, split_dot
.input earlier, visit_index, ast_depth, dfs_first, dfs_first_memo

// ---- matcher atoms ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Body forms that hand a node to the pattern matcher. Not Soufflé: addition 1.
//
// n ~ `pattern`   n matches a code pattern of the policy's language (ast-grep's pattern syntax). Each $X in the
//                 pattern binds the Datalog variable X to the node it matched; $$$X binds X to a list of nodes,
//                 usable only in fix templates. n may be bound or not.
//                 e.g. c ~ `exec($ARG)` matches calls of exec and binds ARG to the argument node.
//                 Variables bound by patterns are written in capitals by convention; nothing else is.
// text_rule(d, {…})  a document-level regex rule (forbid.regex), from a translated JSON rule
// ast(n, { … })   a JSON matcher term with no Datalog form yet; compiled to a transducer program
// Cost: a pattern is tried only at candidates the anchor index picks by kind and by the pattern's literal text;
// a pattern with no literal (`$F($$$)`) is tried at every node of its kind.

// ---- order and aggregation: additions 5 and 6 -------------------------------------------------------------------
// 5. join, an ordered string aggregate. Soufflé's aggregates are count, sum, min, max and mean; none builds a string.
//      s = join x by k sep ", " : { r(k, x) }
//    s is every x of the rows, ordered by k (ties by x), separated by ", "; "" when there are none. Symbols
//    compare by Unicode code point and numbers by value, as Python's sorted() does. `by k` may be
//    a tuple, `by (a, b)`. Checks need it where text is part of a key or of a signed message: a sorted list in a
//    ratchet identity, or the canonical JSON an allowlist signature covers. Message text alone does not need it
//    (the findings contract leaves messages free), but may use it.
//
// 6. ordered by earlier: recursion through `!` and aggregates, along the visit order.
//      .decl owner_code(n: node, x: symbol, c: symbol) ordered by earlier
//    Soufflé rejects a relation that depends on itself through `!` or an aggregate. A few checks need exactly
//    that: a name's state at n is set by the latest earlier write *that qualifies*, and whether a write
//    qualifies depends on the state where it happens. "x's code is the code of its latest assignment whose
//    value has a code" (rebinding to a value without one keeps the old code), where the value's code may come
//    from another name's state at that assignment. "Latest qualifying" is a max over the recursive relation.
//    It is well founded: every step goes to an earlier node. A relation declared `ordered by earlier` may be
//    used under `!` or in an aggregate in its own recursion when the use's first column m is tied to the head's
//    first column n by earlier(m, n) in the same body (inside the aggregate when the use is inside it). The
//    checker enforces this. The engine evaluates such a relation per document in visit order, so rows at n
//    depend only on rows already final.
//    Most state needs no ordered by. When every write resets the name (any assignment clears it, as Python's
//    dict assignment does), "latest write before n" is a max over the writes, which are syntax; the value
//    that write stores then depends positively on the state there, which plain recursion allows. "Every branch
//    raises" is a chain over the branches (python/flow's always_raises). Use ordered by only when which write
//    counts depends on the recursion.

// ---- lattices and demand (Soufflé's, as the engine reads them) --------------------------------------------------
// A relation's last column may be a lattice value: `.decl cv(n: node, v: flat<>)` keeps one value per key, the
// join of every value derived for it (flat<>: one value, or top when two differ; min<> and max<> keep the least
// or greatest). `.lattice L<base> { Lub -> @f }` names a lattice by its join functor (shortlex_min<> keeps the
// shortest, then least, symbol). `widen k` after a lattice .decl sends a value to top once it has changed k
// times, which a lattice of unbounded height needs; `top` in a term is the lattice's top. Typing's constant
// values and qualified names are lattice columns.
// `.pragma "magic-transform" "a, b"` computes a and b only for the arguments rules ask about ("*": every
// relation that can be); no other .pragma is read.
// Aggregates are Soufflé's count, min, max and sum, over a variable or a term (`sum strlen(v) : { … }`);
// Soufflé's substr, to_float, `%`, min/2 and max/2 are built in. `<` on symbols compares their UTF-8 bytes.
// Names starting `rr_` belong to the engine; no rule may declare one, nor redeclare a name this file declares.

// ---- typing programs ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Call targets and types are computed by typing programs: .rr programs the engine runs over the repository before
// the rules, file by file and then in bounded cross-file rounds (typing/programs/ beside this site). A rule reads
// a program's output `o` by declaring `typing_<program>__<o>`, with each node column written as four columns
// (path, kind, start byte, end byte); declaring it is what makes the engine run the program. The libraries do
// the join back to nodes, so rules read typed relations: python/calls (call_target, call_open) and python/types
// (type_of, type_open, proven_type, possible_type, possible_open). Answers are three-valued: a missing row with
// the open relation set means unknown, not false. A round cut short before the fixpoint marks every call it left
// without a target open, so an unfinished answer is never read as "calls nothing".
// Calls of names the workspace does not define are typed from declared returns: `rerail.types/v1` stub files and
// dependency summaries.

// ---- value functions and captures --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Soufflé built-ins used here: cat(a, b) concatenation, strlen(s), to_number(s), to_string(n), contains(a, b),
// and match(re, s).
//
// match("re", s)  s matches the regex re as a whole, as in Soufflé: write `.*` where the text may go on, so
//                 prefix("test_", name) is "starts with test_". On a node or span, s is its text.
//                 It runs on the vocabulary: tested once per distinct interned string, not once per node.
//                 Addition 2, captures: a named group (?<X>…) binds the variable X to what it matched, from
//                 the first match (leftmost, then as the regex engine resolves alternatives).
//                   On a node or span, X is a span: it has text and line, can be a finding's `at`, and can be
//                   the target of an edit. On a symbol, X is a symbol.
//                   A group that takes no part in the match (an unmatched `(?<X>…)?`) gives no row.
//                   Inside `!` or `count : { … }`, groups bind only there, like any variable.
//                 e.g. match("use_(?<x>.*)", "use_foo") binds x = "foo".
// match_all("re", s)  one row per non-overlapping match of re anywhere in s, with that match's groups bound.
// match_all("re", s, ordinal, start, end) over a symbol also binds zero-based occurrence and character offsets.
//                 start is inclusive and end exclusive. Equal captures retain separate occurrences.
//                 This positional form runs each regex once per demanded distinct text, in the same batch.
//
// User functors, declared as in Soufflé and written @name(…). They compute a symbol from symbols.
.functor dot(a: symbol, b: symbol): symbol               // "a.b" ("" . b gives b): @dot("os", "path") = "os.path"
// @qname("react", "memo") = "react#memo": a symbol in a TypeScript module. `#` because TS module names contain
// dots and slashes.
.functor qname(m: symbol, x: symbol): symbol
// @pkg_up("a.b.c", 1) = "a.b": q with its last k dotted segments removed ("" past the root). Resolves Python
// relative imports (`from .. import x`).
.functor pkg_up(q: symbol, k: number): symbol
.functor head(q: symbol): symbol                         // the first dotted segment: @head("os.path") = "os"
// @join(@dir("src/a/b.ts"), "../c") = "src/c": path parent; join and normalise (".." resolved). Resolves
// `import "../c"` from src/a/b.ts.
.functor dir(p: symbol): symbol
.functor join(dir: symbol, rel: symbol): symbol
// @glob_sub("@/*", "src/*", "@/lib/x") = "src/lib/x": tsconfig `paths`. s is matched against pat and its *
// substituted into to; "" if it does not match.
.functor glob_sub(pat: symbol, to: symbol, s: symbol): symbol
// @package_of("react-router-dom/server") = "react-router-dom/server" (subpath kept), "@a/b/c" -> "@a/b/c".
// Names the package entry point a bare specifier imports, as the target symbol's module.
.functor package_of(spec: symbol): symbol
// Transforms: today's JSON `transform` computes a metavariable from another one's text. Each kind is one functor.
// @replace_all("a  b", "\\s+", " ") = "a b": every match of re becomes by; ${1} in by is group 1.
.functor replace_all(s: symbol, re: symbol, by: symbol): symbol
// @convert("max-age", "camelCase", "dash") = "maxAge". Cases: lowerCase, upperCase, capitalize, camelCase,
// snakeCase, kebabCase, pascalCase. seps lists separators ("dash,underscore"); "" splits on all of them.
.functor convert(s: symbol, to: symbol, seps: symbol): symbol
// @slice("_name", 1, 5) = "name": characters start to end-1; negative positions count from the end.
.functor slice(s: symbol, start: number, end: number): symbol
.functor to_precision(n: symbol, digits: number): symbol  // JavaScript's Number.prototype.toPrecision
// @lower("Foo") = "foo": JavaScript's String.prototype.toLowerCase (Unicode default case mapping), for
// case-insensitive comparisons (ESLint's ignoreCase). @convert's lowerCase also splits words.
.functor lower(s: symbol): symbol
// Semver, as npm's node-semver reads ranges: engines, peer ranges, versions.
// @semver_valid(r) = 1 when r parses as a range (node-semver's validRange, loose: "^v16.17.0" is valid), else 0.
.functor semver_valid(r: symbol): number
// @semver_subset(a, b) = 1 when every version range a admits, b admits too (node-semver's subset), else 0:
// subset(">=18", ">=16") = 1. Defined only for two valid ranges: on an invalid one it is an evaluation error
// (the row is Unknown, never 0), so callers guard both arguments with @semver_valid.
.functor semver_subset(a: symbol, b: symbol): number
//
// Places. @place("api/LAYER_GROUPS.yaml", 1) is a span at line 1 of that path, and @place(path, 0) the file
// itself. It is how a finding sits where there is no node: a missing required file, an allowlist, a baseline,
// a Markdown line. The path need not exist. text is "" and docof has no row.
.functor place(path: symbol, line: number): span
//
// Text for keys and signatures.
.functor count_matches(re: symbol, s: symbol): number    // non-overlapping matches of re anywhere in s
.functor sha256(s: symbol): symbol                       // hex digest of s's UTF-8 bytes
// @json_str(s): s as a JSON string literal, quotes included, escaped as Python's json.dumps does by default
// (non-ASCII as \uXXXX, surrogate pairs above U+FFFF), so rebuilt messages match the bytes that were signed.
.functor json_str(s: symbol): symbol
// Python 3.13 repr of scalar Unicode text: quote choice and printable/control character escapes.
.functor python_repr(s: symbol): symbol
.functor nfkc(s: symbol): symbol                         // Unicode NFKC, as Python normalises identifiers
// Python 3.13 decimal int syntax (Unicode 15.1), clamped to inclusive i64 bounds.
// Accepts surrounding Python integer whitespace, signs, and digit-separated underscores.
// max_digits counts digits, excluding separators; zero disables the limit.
// Invalid text, bounds, or a negative limit gives no value. Huge integers need no allocation.
.functor python_int_clamped(s: symbol, lower: number, upper: number, max_digits: number): number
// @ed25519_ok(key_hex, message, sig_b64) = 1 when sig (base64) is key's Ed25519 signature of message's UTF-8
// bytes, else 0. With join and @json_str a rule rebuilds the canonical message a repository signs, whatever its
// layout, and verifies it: lib/data-layer/signing.rr does this for the data layer's two layouts.
.functor ed25519_ok(key: symbol, message: symbol, sig: symbol): number
// @tree_digest(n): a structural tree digest. It does not reproduce CPython AST identity or dump bytes.
.functor tree_digest(n: node): symbol
// Existing hashes of CPython ast.dump text require exact native serialization and field defaults.
// A structural digest is a different input contract. Do not re-sign repository inputs to replace those hashes.
// @unparse(n): n printed canonically by a native printer (comments dropped, quotes and spacing normalised), for
// message previews such as "{@unparse(call)}". Keys and signatures must use their specified canonical format.
.functor unparse(n: node): symbol

// ---- repository inputs (native readers, one derivation per file) ---------------------------------------------
// Facts from the repository that are not source code. Each reader parses one kind of file (JSON configs,
// the Git tree, the policy) and is native because that is IO and format parsing, not logic.
// file("src/c.tsx"): the file exists. Import resolution tests candidate paths against this.
.decl file(path: symbol)                                 // a file in the scanned tree (Git tree facts)
// tsconfig("web", "web/src") for web/tsconfig.json with "baseUrl": "src". `extends` chains are merged first.
.decl tsconfig(dir: symbol, base_url: symbol)            // tsconfig.json / jsconfig.json, `extends` followed
// tsconfig_for("web/src/a.ts", "web"): the nearest tsconfig.json in or above its directory.
.decl tsconfig_for(path: symbol, dir: symbol)            // the tsconfig directory that governs a file
// "paths": {"@/*": ["src/*"]} in web/tsconfig.json gives tsconfig_path("web", "@/*", "src/*"), one row per target.
.decl tsconfig_path(dir: symbol, pattern: symbol, target: symbol)
// package("packages/ui", "@acme/ui") from packages/ui/package.json: maps a workspace package name to its directory.
.decl package(dir: symbol, name: symbol)                 // package.json name
// "exports": {"./button": "./src/button.ts"} in @acme/ui's package.json gives
// package_export("@acme/ui", "./button", "./src/button.ts").
.decl package_export(name: symbol, subpath: symbol, target: symbol)
// package_for("web/src/a.js", "web"): the nearest package.json in or above a file's directory, as tsconfig_for.
// package_field("web", "engines/node", ">=18"): its string values, one row each. The key is the member's path
// from the root, segments joined by "/" and escaped as JSON pointer tokens (RFC 6901: "~" as "~0", "/" as "~1"),
// array elements by index from 0: "type", "engines/node", "devEngines/runtime/0/version". Only strings give
// rows: `"engines": { "node": 18 }` gives none, as the engine ignores a non-string there.
.decl package_for(path: symbol, dir: symbol)
.decl package_field(dir: symbol, key: symbol, v: symbol)
// Python: in `cast("Foo", x)` or `TypeVar("T", bound="Foo")`, the string is a type. Native because the list of
// positions follows Ruff's own table, not anything visible in the tree.
.decl string_annotation(s: node)                         // a string Ruff reads as a type definition
// option("max_depth", "3"): a policy's tunable, so one rule text serves repositories with different settings.
.decl option(name: symbol, v: symbol)  // a rule option's configured value (rerail.policy/v1 `options`)
// option_item("memberSyntaxSortOrder", 0, "none"): element i of a list-valued option, from 0, so its order
// is kept. A boolean option is "true" or "false"; a list option has no `option` row.
.decl option_item(name: symbol, i: number, v: symbol)
// signed_list("allow/exec.txt", "scripts/build.py"): approved exceptions; the signature check is native so a
// rule cannot accept an edited list. A list whose signature fails has no rows, and an input_error row instead.
.decl signed_list(path: symbol, entry: symbol)  // entries of a signed allowlist; refused if the signature fails
// git_tracked("docs/a.md"): the file is in the Git index. file() is what is on disk in the scanned tree; a check
// about committed files needs the difference.
.decl git_tracked(path: symbol)
// git_rev("head", sha), git_rev("base", sha): the revision under check and the one it is compared against (the
// merge base of a change). git_has(sha, path): the path exists at that revision; sha may be any commit
// reachable from head, not only these two. git_commit(sha): sha names a commit reachable from head, so a
// check can verify a commit a document cites.
.decl git_rev(name: symbol, sha: symbol)
.decl git_has(sha: symbol, path: symbol)
.decl git_commit(sha: symbol)
// Python string constants as CPython builds them. str_part(lit, i, v, at, spec): piece i of the str Constant(s)
// that literal lit becomes: adjacent literals are one constant, joined; an f-string gives one piece per literal
// run between interpolations, and spec = 1 for a piece inside a format spec. v is decoded (escapes applied).
// at is the source piece it starts in. Bytes literals give no row. Decoded natively, by the language reference's
// literal rules (escapes, raw prefixes, `{{`/`}}`), checked once against CPython's own decoder offline.
.decl str_part(lit: node, i: number, v: symbol, at: node, spec: number)
// Exact start of each Constant: physical line from 1, UTF-8 byte column from 0.
// Keep `at` above for owner traversal; it is not the fragment's exact coordinate.
// Maximal decoded Python Unicode 15.1 word runs (isalnum or underscore), per Constant.
// Surrogates delimit runs. This projection does not replace full decoded string values.
.decl str_part_word(lit: node, i: number, w: symbol, at: node, spec: number)
.decl str_part_start(lit: node, i: number, l: number, c: number)
// Absolute UTF-8 byte start of the same piece. Joins directly to node byte ranges, including format specifications.
.decl str_part_offset(lit: node, i: number, offset: number)
.input file, tsconfig, tsconfig_for, tsconfig_path, package, package_export, package_for, package_field
.input string_annotation, option, option_item
.input signed_list, git_tracked, git_rev, git_has, git_commit, str_part, str_part_word, str_part_start, str_part_offset
//
// Policy files. `.input r(IO=…, filename="…", …)` reads a repository file the policy names. One reader per
// format; r's columns are filled by name or position. A file that is missing, unreadable or malformed gives no
// rows and one input_error row, so a rule (or `.on input_error`) can report it. Readers only read: signatures
// are checked in Datalog with @ed25519_ok, so any signing layout works without a new reader.
//   IO=file      tab-separated lines (Soufflé's own format)
//   IO=json      path="/counts" (a JSON pointer, default the whole file): an object gives one (key, value) row
//                per member, a list one row per element, a scalar one row; records=true makes each element of a
//                list of objects one row, a column per key
//                A `*` step in path matches every element or member: path="/groups/*/operations/*" gives one
//                row per operation of every group, and each `*` it crossed is a leading column (its index or key).
//                `*#` adds the zero-based member position before its key or index. Object positions follow
//                insertion order, including empty members; a repeated key keeps its first position and last value.
//                projection="type" returns one type-name column for each selected value; projection="json"
//                returns one serialized JSON column for complete DTO transport. Wildcard captures stay in front.
//                Both use the same decoded document as the typed rows. JSON numbers retain their full precision.
//   IO=jsonl     one JSON value per line (JSON Lines); each line is read as IO=json reads a file, with the line
//                number as a leading column
//   IO=yaml      the same shapes as json
//   IO=lines     one row per line: (line number, text); dir="allow/" reads every file of a directory, with
//                (file, line, text) rows, as strict allowlist fragment directories need
//   at="base"    read the file as it is at git_rev("base", …). Burn-down lists that may only shrink compare the
//                two readings.
//   artifact="name"  instead of filename=: a file a CI step wrote, not a repository file (below).
//
// Run artifacts. Rerail never runs repository code. Facts only a run can produce (pytest's collection, behaviour
// probes, a registry built at import time) come from a file the repository's CI writes and `rerail facts stamp`
// wraps with a digest of its inputs. The policy declares the inputs:
//   .artifact "test_collection" inputs "**/*.py", "pyproject.toml"
// An artifact whose inputs changed since it was stamped is stale and gives no rows. Its problems are
// input_error("artifact:NAME", kind, …) rows with kind missing, stale, failed or malformed. Rules that read
// the artifact through `!` or an aggregate are withheld then, and the places it could reach are Unknown
// (data-layer/FINDINGS-LAYER.md, section 2). The format and the CI steps: data-layer/RUN-FACTS.md.
.decl input_error(path: symbol, kind: symbol, detail: symbol)  // missing, unreadable, malformed, signature, stale, failed
.input input_error

// ---- embedded languages (proposed) ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Code inside code (SQL in a Python string, a template's expressions, a string annotation) is to be a guest
// document: parsed with its own grammar, with its own nodes, and declared by libraries rather than built in.
//   guest(g, host, at, grammar)   document g is the text at `at` of document host, parsed as grammar
//   doc_role(d, r)                "primary" (a file), "program" or "fragment" (a guest)
//   host_node(n, h)               h is the innermost host node spanning guest node n
//   root(d, n)                    the root of document d; for a fragment, the expression inside its wrapper
//   embed(at, grammar, role)      a library rule that declares a guest (embeds of embeds up to depth 4)
// parsed(s, g, root) becomes a view over guest and root, and `.scope embedded false` keeps a policy to primary
// documents. A tape may also become a value: `@parse(grammar, text)` and, for fixes, `@splice` and `@unparse`.
// Built as decisions 0055 and 0058 (the guests section above); `.guests off` is the `.scope` here.

// ---- edits (native applier) -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Addition 4. A fix is a set of edits. Rules derive edits like any other rows: a rule ending in `=> e1, e2`
// contributes those edits to the fix in its head (see the "Fixes" section of the syntax page). These four are
// the only edits the binary applies; every other edit (ensure_import, remove_alias, hoist, rename, unwrap, …)
// is Datalog in a library and expands to these.
//
// The applier is native because it works on bytes, not facts. For one fix it:
//   1. turns each edit into a byte range in the edited node's file (a fix may edit several files);
//   2. fills templates: in `…`, $x is variable x's value (a node's or span's source text, or a symbol); $$$X is a
//      list variable bound by a pattern; the text is reindented to where it lands, as ast-grep's fix does;
//   2b. pads replace and insert texts as Ruff's `pad` and ESLint's fixers do: when the text would start (end)
//      with an identifier character right after (before) an identifier character outside the edit, a space goes
//      in between, so `return{…}` → `return dict(…)` and `d.keys()in x` → `d in x`, never `returndict` or `din`.
//      Rules write the plain text and never pad by hand;
//   3. drops the whole fix if two of its edits overlap, or the same node gets two different texts;
//   4. applies all files or none, reparses them, and drops the fix if any no longer parses.
// After a fix is applied, Rerail re-checks the edited files; the finding must be gone (checked in rule tests).
//
// replace(n, `$A or $B`): n's source (a node or a captured span) becomes the text.
.edit replace(n: loc, text: symbol)                      // built in
// delete(n): remove n. In a list (arguments, parameters, import names, JSX children, dict entries) one separator
// goes with it; a statement alone on its lines takes its lines with it, so no blank line is left behind.
.edit delete(n: loc)                                     // built in
// insert_before(n, `x = 1`) / insert_after(n, …): new text as n's sibling. Before or after a statement it goes on
// its own line at n's indentation; in a list it gets a separator.
.edit insert_before(n: node, text: symbol)               // built in
.edit insert_after(n: node, text: symbol)                // built in

// ---- findings and enforcement (directives) -----------------------------------------------------------------------
// Settings of the findings layer, which runs after the rules: rows become keys, files get outcomes, then the gate,
// the budgets and the verdict. Semantics and engine needs: data-layer/FINDINGS-LAYER.md.
// A finding's key is (policy, code, path, line), as the findings contract says. Order, multiplicity and messages
// are free, so two matches at one key are one finding; a check that needs two keeps them apart with a tag.
//
// .violation V(at: loc, …) "message"          at is a node, a span, or a @place. A place with line 0 is a file-level
//                                              finding; @place of a missing path is a repository-level one. The
//                                              code is V.
// .violation V(at: loc, …) code "E501" "…"    a constant code, for a code that is no identifier ("@next/next/x")
//     suppress ruff "E501", …                 continuation line: the tool comments that silence it (`# noqa: E501`;
//                                              ruff, flake8, nosec, eslint, oxlint, biome, clippy, phpcs, phpstan,
//                                              psalm, sqlfluff), one line per tool
//     fix [safe] F "title"                    continuation line: the fix relation F offers (edits, above)
// .violation V(at: loc, c: symbol) code {c}   the code is a column: SEC123 found in a comment is its own code.
//     code {c} in "SEC\d+"                     the codes it may produce; a row outside them is Unknown, not a finding
// .violation V(…, t: symbol) tag {t}          an extra key field: findings at one place stay apart (one per probe)
// .mode block | steer | log                   the policy's default; `.mode log CODE, …` for some codes
// .severity V warning | error                 presentation and `.budget … by severity`; never decides blocking
// .gate V, W                                  when they have keys, those are the report; every other file of the
//                                              policy is Unknown(gated). Gates may not depend on other violations.
// .on syntax_error report CODE [at line 1]    also report each syntax error as a finding. Either way the document
//                                              is withheld (no facts) and its outcome is Unknown(parse).
// .on input_error report CODE                 a repository input that is missing or malformed is a finding.
// .guests on | off                            whether the run language's guests are shown to rules that do not
//                                              reach them through a guest relation (on by default); embeds are
//                                              made either way (decision 0058).
// .on guest_error [GRAMMAR] skip | partial [warn | report CODE]
//                                              a guest that does not parse: skip (the default) has no nodes;
//                                              partial keeps the tree the parser recovered. warn or report also
//                                              gives a finding at the guest's line in its host (warn: severity
//                                              warning). A file's own syntax_error is never a guest's.
// .budget V by code { "A": 3 }                 findings may exist up to these counts without blocking. Counts are of
//                                              distinct keys, suppressed ones included; a group without a ceiling
//                                              has 0. by code | severity | group | path, or total.
// .budget V by code from base [floor] [drop N% [from obs]]
//                                              ceilings are the rows of relation base(code, n), usually a verified
//                                              baseline. floor: counts may not drop either; drop: not by more than
//                                              N% of obs (default the ceilings). An incomplete count can only fail
//                                              upwards, else the budget is Unknown. A bad baseline is a violation
//                                              the policy writes, usually a gate.
//     … report CODE at "path"                  the budget's own finding (default code V.budget, no path)
//                                              A ratchet on a metric (a count of annotations, of tests) is not a
//                                              budget: it is a rule comparing the metric with its ceiling.
// .group errors = "A", "B"                    names a set of codes, for `.budget … by group`.
// .artifact "name" inputs "glob", …           a run artifact and the files it is built from (Run artifacts above).
// .scope report dirs "api", "impl"            where findings may land; facts still come from the whole scope,
//                                              so imports and manifests outside it are read.
// .scope text "**/*.md", "**/*.rs"            documents read as text only (source, src_line; no tree).
// .scope dirs option("application_root")     a scope entry taken from a policy option, so one policy text serves
//                                              repositories laid out differently (the scripts' --application-root).
// .requires "data-layer:a", "data-layer:b"    a suite policy: one finding per member policy that fails, or that
//                                              ends unknown on any file. Members run as usual.
// .scope tests false                          drops documents with a `test` or `tests` path part. Walks always
//                                              skip .git, .venv, venv, __pycache__ and tmp.
